{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-79932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/2}$ is a solution to the equation $4{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2})+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}-2u{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{e}^{x/2}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x/2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f618a7b7265dc697a1ece5388b798c8085a334db57242cda61082c9068799b7"} {"question" "q-79932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/4}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{-x/2}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{x/4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79932" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-3x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+2(u{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+8{u}^{′}{x}^{3}+4u{x}^{2}-3{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{2}+2u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2x{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({x}^{-3/2}){x}^{2}={x}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f618a7b7265dc697a1ece5388b798c8085a334db57242cda61082c9068799b7"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-3/2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{3/2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-79935" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f618a7b7265dc697a1ece5388b798c8085a334db57242cda61082c9068799b7"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+Bx"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{3}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-90763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{4t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+4u{e}^{4t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t})-16u{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+8w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-8$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-8t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-8\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f618a7b7265dc697a1ece5388b798c8085a334db57242cda61082c9068799b7"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{4t}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4t}+Bt{e}^{4t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{t}"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{t}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-129957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-339879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=-12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-339879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-2x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-2{e}^{-2x})^{′}=4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(4{e}^{-2x})+5(-2{e}^{-2x})-6({e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-2x})^{′}={e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})^{′}=-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x})+5({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})-6(x{e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069363/state.json" "file_sha256" "86d84e8bdcd6328bd254f2130a8af8e0c5c6b267e572065e7802b30e5ec3eac3"} {"question" "q-339879" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/